@anon.152f57749fcdf0a673d809c4f4545e7f.589 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1147 x i8] }> <{ [1147 x i8] c"Provides access to the current outer container's (or page's, if none) size\0A(width and height).\0A\0AThe given function must accept a single parameter, `size`, which is a\0Adictionary with keys `width` and `height`, both of type [`length`].\0A\0A```example\0A#let text = lorem(30)\0A#layout(size => [\0A  #let (height,) = measure(\0A    block(width: size.width, text),\0A  )\0A  This text is #height high with\0A  the current page width: \\\0A  #text\0A])\0A```\0A\0AIf the `layout` call is placed inside of a box width a width of `{800pt}`\0Aand a height of `{400pt}`, then the specified function will be given the\0Aparameter `{(width: 800pt, height: 400pt)}`. If it placed directly into the\0Apage it receives the page's dimensions minus its margins. This is mostly\0Auseful in combination with [measurement]($measure).\0A\0AYou can also use this function to resolve [`ratio`] to fixed lengths. This\0Amight come in handy if you're building your own layout abstractions.\0A\0A```example\0A#layout(size => {\0A  let half = 50% * size.width\0A  [Half a page is #half wide.]\0A})\0A```\0A\0ANote that this function will provide an infinite width or height if one of\0Athe page width or height is `auto`, respectively." }>, align 1
